This is a cross-post in Markdown format that you can find here:
https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/ann-uniker-nl-unikernels-in-ocaml/18625

We are delighted to announce the launch of our new website:
[uniker.nl][uniker.nl]. This website embodies our new approach to developin=
g
unikernels with OCaml 5. It offers a new way of developing unikernels and
now
places just as much emphasis on the deployment and use of unikernels. The
website features a comprehensive [tutorial][uniker.nl-tutorial] and
resources
to help you get to grips with our new ecosystem, providing a _first entry
point_ for anyone interested in unikernels in OCaml.

The content of this site is available [here][uniker.nl-repo], is powered by
the
excellent [YOCaml][yocaml] project, and is deployed using our
[immuable][immuable] unikernel.

## Our new ecosystem

We were able to present our new workflow at the [OCaml Workshop
2026][workshop]
([slides][slides]) and our approach can be summarised in two main points:
- the development of unikernels
- the deployment of unikernels and their uses

### To develop unikernels

Our co-operative has been developing unikernels for quite a few years now.
[Our
blog][blog-robur] showcases some of the ones we use for our infrastructure.
Based on our experience, we are well aware of the constraints and
limitations
of what we can achieve when it comes to developing an application in the
form
of a unikernel. The best-known constraint we have accepted is not to requir=
e
the POSIX interface (nor to attempt to replicate it) in terms of protocols
and
formats. One of the rules still adhered to today is, amongst other things,
[not
to depend on I/O or a scheduler][sans-io]<sup>[1][sans-io-2]</sup> when
providing implementations.

Building on this experience, we have been able to develop protocols and
formats
that are currently being used by others in contexts other than unikernels.

However, we have also seen the limits of what we can achieve, particularly
with
what MirageOS currently offers. With this in mind, and upon further
reflection
on the constraints we face when developing unikernels (particularly
regarding
cross-compilation), we have decided to structure the development of
unikernels
using two tools: [`unic`][unic] and [`mfetch`][mfetch].

#### [`unic`][unic]: how to infer dependencies and _vendor_ them

One constraint we face with unikernels is the need to recompile C files wit=
h
specific options so that they do not depend on what already exists on the
host
system. The approach taken by MirageOS 3 was to distribute the result of
this
specific compilation within libraries that contain C stubs (such as
[`digestif`][digestif] or [`mirage-crypto`][mirage-crypto]). The problem is
that we were forcing users of these libraries to install
[`ocaml-solo5`][ocaml-solo5] in order to obtain a compilation environment
for C
files specific to [Solo5][solo5], our core component for producing
unikernels.

MirageOS 4 then arrived with `dune` and its contexts, allowing us to
achieve a
consistent compilation environment for C files. However, this approach
introduced new a constraint: all dependencies of our unikernels had to use
`dune`, which is **legitimately** not the case in the OCaml ecosystem.

It is for this reason that we decided to refine the need to recompile some
of
our libraries a little further and, finally, to be able to capitalise on a
key
advantage of [`ocaml-solo5`][ocaml-solo5], namely that compiling OCaml file=
s
with this compiler produces exactly the same result as if you were using
your
host OCaml compiler: in other words, and as has been the case from the
beginning, we strictly only need to cross-compile the C files.

It is therefore at the intersection of these two features (only needing to
cross-compile the C files and being able to reuse libraries written in pure
OCaml and compiled with the host compiler) that we can offer users the
ability
to write this sort of code without involving any maintenance work on
libraries
that do not use `dune` and without necessarily having to recompile
everything:

```shell
$ opam install unic
$ cat >main.ml<<EOF
> open Digestif

> let () =3D Mkernel.run [] @@ fun () ->
>   Fmt.pr "%a\n%!" SHA1.pp (SHA1.digest_string "Hello World!")
> EOF
$ unic infer -r .
Module Digestif is provided by several ocamlfind packages:
  [0] digestif.c
  [1] digestif.ocaml
Pick one [0-1]: 0
digestif
mkernel
$ opam source digestif
$ opam source mkernel
$ dune build
$ solo5-hvt -- _build/solo5/main.exe --solo5:quiet
2ef7bde608ce5404e97d5f042f95f89f1c232871Solo5
```

`unic` is therefore a tool capable of inferring the dependencies of an OCam=
l
project without using a specific build system, and of calculating what
needs to
be cross-compiled (in this case `digestif` and `mkernel` but not `fmt`) to
produce a unikernel. The project serves our purpose first and foremost, but
it
is worth noting that it can also be useful when compiling statically linked
executables.

This tool offers a whole range of sub-tools for analysing META files,
viewing
an artefact from different perspectives (its module name, its `ocamlfind`
package name and its OPAM package name) or analysing OCaml artefacts (such
as
`*.cmxa` files).

We would particularly like to thank @Octachron for his [`codept`][codept]
project (on which `unic` is based) and for working with us to improve it.

### [`mfetch`][mfetch]: how to vendor dependencies

Having identified more precisely which dependencies need to be
cross-compiled,
we now need to download these dependencies. Although `opam source` does the
job
very well, we wanted a tool that could download the source code from variou=
s
types of source, such as:
- from a _tarball_, as is usually the case
- but also from a Git repository
- or even from a specific location on your file system

That is why we developed `mfetch`, which allows you to download software
sources based on information provided by OPAM or the user, and to create a
`vendors` folder containing all these sources.

```shell
