/uniker.nl">uniker.nl</a>]. This website embodies our new approach to devel=
oping<br>unikernels with OCaml 5. It offers a new way of developing unikern=
els and now<br>places just as much emphasis on the deployment and use of un=
ikernels. The<br>website features a comprehensive [tutorial][uniker.nl-tuto=
rial] and resources<br>to help you get to grips with our new ecosystem, pro=
viding a _first entry<br>point_ for anyone interested in unikernels in OCam=
l.<br><br>The content of this site is available [here][uniker.nl-repo], is =
powered by the<br>excellent [YOCaml][yocaml] project, and is deployed using=
 our<br>[immuable][immuable] unikernel.<br><br>## Our new ecosystem<br><br>=
We were able to present our new workflow at the [OCaml Workshop 2026][works=
hop]<br>([slides][slides]) and our approach can be summarised in two main p=
oints:<br>- the development of unikernels<br>- the deployment of unikernels=
 and their uses<br><br>### To develop unikernels<br><br>Our co-operative ha=
s been developing unikernels for quite a few years now. [Our<br>blog][blog-=
robur] showcases some of the ones we use for our infrastructure.<br>Based o=
n our experience, we are well aware of the constraints and limitations<br>o=
f what we can achieve when it comes to developing an application in the for=
m<br>of a unikernel. The best-known constraint we have accepted is not to r=
equire<br>the POSIX interface (nor to attempt to replicate it) in terms of =
protocols and<br>formats. One of the rules still adhered to today is, among=
st other things, [not<br>to depend on I/O or a scheduler][sans-io]&lt;sup&g=
t;[1][sans-io-2]&lt;/sup&gt; when<br>providing implementations.<br><br>Buil=
ding on this experience, we have been able to develop protocols and formats=
<br>that are currently being used by others in contexts other than unikerne=
ls.<br><br>However, we have also seen the limits of what we can achieve, pa=
rticularly with<br>what MirageOS currently offers. With this in mind, and u=
pon further reflection<br>on the constraints we face when developing uniker=
nels (particularly regarding<br>cross-compilation), we have decided to stru=
cture the development of unikernels<br>using two tools: [`unic`][unic] and =
[`mfetch`][mfetch].<br><br>#### [`unic`][unic]: how to infer dependencies a=
nd _vendor_ them<br><br>One constraint we face with unikernels is the need =
to recompile C files with<br>specific options so that they do not depend on=
 what already exists on the host<br>system. The approach taken by MirageOS =
3 was to distribute the result of this<br>specific compilation within libra=
ries that contain C stubs (such as<br>[`digestif`][digestif] or [`mirage-cr=
ypto`][mirage-crypto]). The problem is<br>that we were forcing users of the=
se libraries to install<br>[`ocaml-solo5`][ocaml-solo5] in order to obtain =
a compilation environment for C<br>files specific to [Solo5][solo5], our co=
re component for producing unikernels.<br><br>MirageOS 4 then arrived with =
`dune` and its contexts, allowing us to achieve a<br>consistent compilation=
 environment for C files. However, this approach<br>introduced new a constr=
aint: all dependencies of our unikernels had to use<br>`dune`, which is **l=
egitimately** not the case in the OCaml ecosystem.<br><br>It is for this re=
ason that we decided to refine the need to recompile some of<br>our librari=
es a little further and, finally, to be able to capitalise on a key<br>adva=
ntage of [`ocaml-solo5`][ocaml-solo5], namely that compiling OCaml files<br=
>with this compiler produces exactly the same result as if you were using y=
our<br>host OCaml compiler: in other words, and as has been the case from t=
he<br>beginning, we strictly only need to cross-compile the C files.<br><br=
>It is therefore at the intersection of these two features (only needing to=
<br>cross-compile the C files and being able to reuse libraries written in =
pure<br>OCaml and compiled with the host compiler) that we can offer users =
the ability<br>to write this sort of code without involving any maintenance=
 work on libraries<br>that do not use `dune` and without necessarily having=
 to recompile everything:<br><br>```shell<br>$ opam install unic<br>$ cat &=
