br><br>&gt; let () =3D Mkernel.run [] @@ fun () -&gt;<br>&gt; =C2=A0 Fmt.pr=
 &quot;%a\n%!&quot; SHA1.pp (SHA1.digest_string &quot;Hello World!&quot;)<b=
r>&gt; EOF<br>$ unic infer -r .<br>Module Digestif is provided by several o=
camlfind packages:<br>=C2=A0 [0] digestif.c<br>=C2=A0 [1] digestif.ocaml<br=
>Pick one [0-1]: 0<br>digestif<br>mkernel<br>$ opam source digestif<br>$ op=
am source mkernel<br>$ dune build<br>$ solo5-hvt -- _build/solo5/main.exe -=
-solo5:quiet<br>2ef7bde608ce5404e97d5f042f95f89f1c232871Solo5<br>```<br><br=
>`unic` is therefore a tool capable of inferring the dependencies of an OCa=
ml<br>project without using a specific build system, and of calculating wha=
t needs to<br>be cross-compiled (in this case `digestif` and `mkernel` but =
not `fmt`) to<br>produce a unikernel. The project serves our purpose first =
and foremost, but it<br>is worth noting that it can also be useful when com=
piling statically linked<br>executables.<br><br>This tool offers a whole ra=
nge of sub-tools for analysing META files, viewing<br>an artefact from diff=
erent perspectives (its module name, its `ocamlfind`<br>package name and it=
s OPAM package name) or analysing OCaml artefacts (such as<br>`*.cmxa` file=
s).<br><br>We would particularly like to thank @Octachron for his [`codept`=
][codept]<br>project (on which `unic` is based) and for working with us to =
improve it.<br><br>### [`mfetch`][mfetch]: how to vendor dependencies<br><b=
r>Having identified more precisely which dependencies need to be cross-comp=
