ocked into _mfetch.locked<br>```<br><br>#### Libraries for OCaml 5<br><br>O=
ur new workflow now uses OCaml 5. As well as updating the basic building<br=
>blocks required to produce unikernels with OCaml 5, we have also developed=
 a<br>series of libraries that primarily enable us to take advantage of eff=
ects.<br>Furthermore, over the past several years we have developed:<br>- [=
Miou][miou-announce], our scheduler for OCaml 5<br>- [`mkernel`][mkernel], =
a derivative of our Miou scheduler designed to handle<br>=C2=A0 the I/O pro=
vided by Solo5<br>- [`mnet`][mnet-announce], a reimplementation of the TCP/=
IP stack (using<br>=C2=A0 [`utcp`][utcp]) in OCaml with effects<br>- and pl=
enty of other libraries such as [`mhttp`][mhttp],<br>=C2=A0 [`msendmail`][m=
sendmail], [`mfat`][mfat], [`vifu`][vifu], etc.<br><br>These libraries, cur=
rently used by some of our deployed unikernels, allow us to<br>offer develo=
pers a _direct-style_ approach to implementing their unikernels.<br><br>But=
 what is key to take away from this emerging ecosystem is the retrospective=
<br>analysis of past projects to ensure we do not repeat the same mistakes.=
 We very<br>quickly realised that it could be very difficult to migrate exi=
sting libraries<br>using [`lwt`][lwt] (the MirageOS scheduler) to Miou (or =
any other scheduler<br>using effects). We therefore took the opportunity no=
t only to propose a<br>_direct-style_ approach using Miou, but also to reth=
ink the implementation of<br>our libraries in light of what we&#39;ve learn=
t from deploying unikernels with<br>MirageOS. We have paid particular atten=
tion to memory usage, to avoiding known<br>CVEs from our previous implement=
ations (as we are also part of the OCaml<br>security team), and also to off=
ering an alternative API that does not<br>systematically rely on functors.<=
/div><div><br></div><div>The approach is still experimental, as we need to =
consolidate this new<br>ecosystem across several layers, but we are firmly =
convinced (based on what<br>we&#39;ve observed in our deployed unikernels) =
that these new implementations are<br>significantly better than what we use=
d in the past.<br><br>### To deploy unikernels<br><br>The other side of uni=
kernels is deployment. Indeed, it is not just a matter of<br>developing uni=
kernels (and making their development easier) but also of<br>deploying and =
using them. Our main objective remains to replace existing<br>services with=
 unikernels (name servers, websites, time servers, etc.), and the<br>way to=
 compete with existing infrastructure is to actively deploy and use our<br>=
unikernels.<br><br>That is why we have developed two projects:<br>- [Albatr=
oss][albatross], an OCaml service for launching unikernels<br>- [Aussi][aus=
si], an OCI runtime for Solo5<br><br>We mainly use Albatross in conjunction=
 with our infrastructure for verifying<br>the reproducibility of our uniker=
nels: [<a href=3D"http://builds.robur.coop">builds.robur.coop</a>][<a href=
=3D"http://builds.robur.coop">builds.robur.coop</a>].<br>In this way, we ca=
n trace the dependencies of the unikernels - particularly<br>their CVEs - a=
nd ensure supply-chain security.<br><br>Next comes deployment, and we are t=
herefore seeking to integrate our unikernels<br>into existing infrastructur=
e and provide ways of monitoring them, in particular<br>using [InfluxDB][in=
fluxdb], [Telegraf][telegraf] and [Grafana][grafana].<br><br>This is how we=
 are able to offer services such as (note that some haven&#39;t yet</div><d=
iv>been ported to `mkernel`/`mnet`, but will be soon):<br>- simple websites=
 such as our <a href=3D"https://blog.robur.coop/">https://blog.robur.coop/<=
/a> (using [`unipi`][unipi]<br>=C2=A0 or [`immuable`][immuable])<br>- domai=
n name management for domains such as `<a href=3D"http://robur.coop">robur.=
coop</a>`, `<a href=3D"http://reyn.ir">reyn.ir</a>` or<br>=C2=A0 `<a href=
=3D"http://din.osau.re">din.osau.re</a>` (via our [primary][dns-primary-git=
] DNS server and<br>=C2=A0 [secondary][mnet-secondary] server)<br>- a VPN s=
ervice, whether using [MirageVPN][MirageVPN] or [Zostera][zostera]<br>- a t=
ime server (using [Chaos][chaos]): <a href=3D"https://chaos.osau.re/">https=
://chaos.osau.re/</a><br>- a [CalDAV][caldav] calendar system<br>- [a maili=
ng list][<a href=3D"http://mailingl.st">mailingl.st</a>] using the [`ptt`][=
ptt] project<br>- a TLS termination proxy such as [`tlstunnel`][tlstunnel] =
or<br>=C2=A0 [`contruno`][contruno] (used by <a href=3D"http://sherlocode.c=
om">sherlocode.com</a>)<br>- and many more!<br><br>It is therefore through =
actual use that we improve our unikernels and the<br>libraries that depend =
on them. That is why part of our tutorial also focuses on<br>deploying unik=
ernels, so that you can use them and help us with your feedback.<br><br>Fin=
ally, we are also proposing a project called [Mollymawk][mollymawk],<br>des=
igned to simplify the deployment of unikernels via a web interface, so that=
,<br>in future, we can offer access to our infrastructure to anyone who wis=
hes to<br>use it (and we would like to thank @kit-ty-kate for their various=
 attempts).<br><br>## About us<br><br>Finally, none of this would be possib=
le without our co-operative and the way we<br>organise our work. You can vi=
ew the details of our finances<br>[here][robur-finance]. We accept donation=
s and have recently secured funding<br>from NLnet. Private companies such a=
s [Ahrefs][ahrefs] and [Tarides][tarides]<br>also sponsor our work. We also=
 have partnerships with [OCamlPro][ocamlpro] and<br>the [OCaml Foundation][=
ocf]. And we contribute to the OCaml ecosystem on a<br>daily basis by worki=
ng with individuals and companies.<br><br>You can find more details on [our=
 website][<a href=3D"http://robur.coop">robur.coop</a>]. We would like to<b=
r>emphasise that it is thanks to our structure (that of an association) tha=
t we<br>are able to carry out this work, and we would like to thank our spo=
nsors.</div><div><br></div><div>## Unikernels and MirageOS<br><br>Enthusias=
ts may wonder what the advantage of our approach is compared to<br>MirageOS=
, a unikernel project that remains active thanks to our maintenance<br>work=
. Our approach is characterised by five points that we have identified as<b=
r>glass ceilings to the adoption of unikernels:<br>- the first concerns the=
 use of [`ocaml-cstruct`][ocaml-cstruct]. The<br>=C2=A0 fundamental issue r=
emains the use of `bigarray` as _buffer_ instead of<br>=C2=A0 `bytes`/`stri=
ng`. Our experience whilst developing [MirageVPN][miragevpn] has<br>=C2=A0 =
shown us that using `cstruct` as a systematic library for the _buffer_<br>=
=C2=A0 concept Across our entire ecosystem was our biggest **penalty** in t=
erms of<br>=C2=A0 throughput for our unikernel. This is why we decided not =
only to develop<br>=C2=A0 [`bstr`][bstr] and [`slice`][bstr], but also to r=
ewrite the majority of our<br>=C2=A0 libraries by replacing `cstruct` with =
`bytes`/`string` (this was the case for<br>=C2=A0 [`ocaml-tls`][ocaml-tls-p=
r], [`mirage-crypto`][mirage-crypto-pr],<br>=C2=A0 [`ocaml-dns`][ocaml-dns-=
pr] and, more recently, [`awa-ssh`][awa-ssh-pr] and<br>=C2=A0 [`utcp`][utcp=
-pr]). The conclusion is quite simple: it is not a question of<br>=C2=A0 st=
opping using bigarray, but rather of not making their use systematic in our=
<br>=C2=A0 aPIs.<br>- the second point concerns the use of functors to expr=
ess the devices required<br>=C2=A0 by the unikernel. The approach of reusin=
g interfaces to inject specific<br>=C2=A0 implementations that concretize t=
hese devices via functors may seem<br>=C2=A0 appealing, but the maintenance=
 cost of such an ecosystem=E2=80=94constrained by<br>=C2=A0 interfaces that=
 are no longer really up to date with new concepts=E2=80=94has led us<br>=
=C2=A0 into [absurd situations][absurd] whenever we&#39;ve needed to develo=
p more<br>=C2=A0 specific unikernels tailored to our requirements. Once aga=
in, the rule is not<br>=C2=A0 to stop using functors, but not to make their=
 use systematic.<br>- the third point concerns the compilation of unikernel=
s. MirageOS relies on a<br>=C2=A0 workaround that, even today, integrates v=
ery poorly with `dune`; this can be<br>=C2=A0 defined as: _multi-stage comp=
ilation_, where a code-generation phase is<br>=C2=A0 required depending on =
the desired _target_. This way of structuring the<br>=C2=A0 workflow with a=
 set of dependencies dictated by the tool explicitly restricts<br>=C2=A0 th=
e developer in what they wish to do and how they wish to do it. Although<br=
>=C2=A0 there are constraints on compiling a unikernel (such as not using t=
he `Unix`<br>=C2=A0 module), our cooperative has decided to provide **infor=
mative** (and not<br>=C2=A0 prescriptive) tools that assist (but do not for=
ce) the user in designing<br>=C2=A0 their unikernel (furthermore, we are al=
so delighted to introduce<br>=C2=A0 [`unic`][unic] and [`mfetch`][mfetch]).=
 The key point of our new approach is<br>=C2=A0 to ensure that it is the us=
er who has the final say, rather than the tool (in<br>=C2=A0 this case, `mi=
rage`), in the development of unikernels.<br>=C2=A0 <br>=C2=A0 It is very i=
ntricate on what the `mirage` tool does, including generation of<br>=C2=A0 =
functor applications and some initialization stubs, as well as opam version=
<br>=C2=A0 constraints (since the functors and initialization functions may=
 change in<br>=C2=A0 new opam packages). The `mirage` tool has a closed wor=
ld assumption, and it<br>=C2=A0 is hard to use a package not known by `mira=
ge` - it is as well very hard to<br>=C2=A0 use `js_of_ocaml` in a `mirage` =
unikernel.<br>- the fourth point concerns the maintenance of this ecosystem=
, and specifically<br>=C2=A0 the maintenance of the tools and libraries on =
which MirageOS relies.<br>=C2=A0 Furthermore, we have experienced some disa=
ppointments with `opam-monorepo`,<br>=C2=A0 and we are finding, day by day,=
 that it is becoming increasingly difficult to<br>=C2=A0 update `mirage-tcp=
ip` in line with OCaml 5. Our involvement in the OCaml<br>=C2=A0 security t=
eam has also led us to wish to deprecate certain libraries due to a<br>=C2=
=A0 lack of maintenance. We are therefore refocusing our ecosystem on what =
our<br>=C2=A0 co-operative produces and maintains.<br>- Finally, the last p=
oint concerns our work with the community, which remains<br>=C2=A0 unchange=
d despite the emergence of AI. We have observed a certain abusive use<br>=
=C2=A0 of AI to solve non-existent problems relating to unikernels, which, =
in our<br>=C2=A0 view, undermines our credibility regarding the security, m=
aintenance and<br>=C2=A0 quality of our software. In this regard, we are tr=
ying to distance ourselves<br>=C2=A0 from those who prefer _vibe-coding_ ra=
ther than deployment. This approach is<br>=C2=A0 reflected in our use of th=
e [AGPL][agpl] as our licence (whilst offering the<br>=C2=A0 option of dual=
 licensing) and a [POLICY][policy] regarding the use of AI.<br><br>All of t=
his has led us to propose MirageOS 5, which represents a fundamental<br>dep=
arture from the factors that characterised the failure of MirageOS 4 (for<b=
r>which I was the release manager). It is therefore, having acknowledged th=
is<br>defeat (and analysed it), that we are proposing a new approach to cre=
ating and<br>deploying unikernels: [<a href=3D"http://uniker.nl">uniker.nl<=
/a>][<a href=3D"http://uniker.nl">uniker.nl</a>].<br><br>As regards the pro=
jects associated with the MirageOS project, we will, of<br>course, continue=
 to maintain some of them, whilst at the same time offering<br>more up-to-d=
ate solutions to those who use some of our libraries. We would also<br>be d=
elighted to help anyone wishing to migrate to our new libraries and tools;<=
br>we are (as we always have been) available to assist.</div><div><br></div=
